Conterm-local, on top of 0002.

An RTL ligature such as lam-alef is one glyph for letters that occupy
two cells. CoreText attributes the glyph to its first letter, so the
cell of the letter it absorbed stays empty and reads as a space inside
the word (سلا م for سلام).

The CoreText shaper now moves such a glyph to the leftmost of its cells
and marks how many it spans; the CoreText face widens the glyph so its
advance fills them. Only cells no other glyph is drawn in are claimed.

